    debrief: 'Seven appointments were scheduled in May 2023. BETWEEN is inclusive on both ends: BETWEEN \'2023-05-01\' AND \'2023-05-31\' catches any date from May 1 through May 31. This works correctly for ISO-format date strings (YYYY-MM-DD) because lexicographic string comparison matches chronological order. Two common alternatives: WHERE strftime(\'%Y-%m\', scheduled_at) = \'2023-05\' (explicit SQLite date function, more readable) and WHERE scheduled_at >= \'2023-05-01\' AND scheduled_at < \'2023-06-01\' (half-open interval, preferred in production to avoid end-of-month edge cases with timestamps). For analytics work, BETWEEN on date-only columns is safe and concise.\n\n**Wrong answer (runs, measures the wrong thing):** BETWEEN \'2023-05-01\' AND \'2023-05-30\' — off by one on the end date. May has 31 days. Any appointment on May 31 is silently excluded. In this datamart no appointments fall on May 31, so the wrong query returns 7 — the correct number — hiding the bug entirely. On real operational data with appointments on the last day of the month, the audit is incomplete. **Catch it:** the safe habit — always use the half-open interval WHERE scheduled_at >= \'2023-05-01\' AND scheduled_at < \'2023-06-01\'. This never has end-of-month off-by-one issues.\n\n**Sanity check:** SELECT COUNT(*) FROM appointments WHERE strftime(\'%Y-%m\', scheduled_at)=\'2023-05\' = 7. This is the most direct verification — no BETWEEN involved, so it\'s an independent cross-check of your date filter.',
