    title: 'Above-Average Enterprise Accounts',
    company: 'HubSpot',
    companyDomain: 'hubspot.com',
    difficulty: 'Medium',
    tags: ['correlated subquery', 'WHERE', 'AVG'],
    roles: ['PA', 'DA', 'PM'],
    priority: 2,
    estimatedMin: 10,
    datamartId: 'saas',
    prompt: 'Product analysts at HubSpot need to identify large accounts within each industry by comparing employee counts against industry averages. Return account_id, company_name, industry, and employee_count for accounts where employee_count exceeds the average employee_count for their industry. Order by industry, employee_count descending.',
    expectedColumns: ['account_id', 'company_name', 'industry', 'employee_count'],
    expectedRowCount: 4,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider how to compare each account\'s employee count to the average of its industry. Think about using a subquery to calculate the average for each industry.', starterCode: 'SELECT account_id, company_name, industry, employee_count FROM accounts WHERE employee_count > (SELECT AVG(employee_count) FROM accounts WHERE industry = \'---industry--\') ORDER BY industry, employee_count DESC;' },
      { text: 'Ensure the subquery correctly filters accounts by industry and calculates the average employee count. Structure your query to return only those accounts that meet the condition.', starterCode: 'SELECT a.account_id, a.company_name, a.industry, a.employee_count FROM accounts a WHERE a.employee_count > (SELECT AVG(a2.employee_count) FROM accounts a2 WHERE a2.industry = a.industry) ORDER BY a.industry, a.employee_count DESC;' }
    ],
    hints: ['What does one row in your result represent? Your output needs 4 rows with columns: account_id, company_name, industry, employee_count.', 'Think about which SQL clause handles the core transformation here.'],
    checkValues: [{ company_name: 'Kilo Pharma', employee_count: '250' }, { company_name: 'Foxtrot Media', employee_count: '40' }],
    solution: 'SELECT a1.account_id, a1.company_name, a1.industry, a1.employee_count\nFROM accounts a1\nWHERE a1.employee_count > (\n  SELECT AVG(a2.employee_count)\n  FROM accounts a2\n  WHERE a2.industry = a1.industry\n)\nORDER BY a1.industry, a1.employee_count DESC',
    debrief: 'Four accounts exceed their industry average: Kilo Pharma (healthcare, 250), Foxtrot Media (media, 40), Blue Retail\'s counterpart Juliet Fashion (retail, 90), and Echo Tech (tech, 300). Single-account industries (logistics, hospitality, manufacturing, food, education) cannot produce any above-average result — a useful edge case to mention in an interview. The correlated subquery re-runs for each outer row; for large tables, rewrite using a window function: AVG(employee_count) OVER (PARTITION BY industry) is equivalent and faster.\n\n**Forensic trap:** Using >= (greater-than-or-equal) instead of > returns accounts exactly at their industry average as well. For this dataset, tech industry average = (150 + 300 + 100) / 3 = 183.3, so no tech account is exactly at the average and the change is invisible in testing. But for the retail industry: Blue Retail (50) and Juliet Fashion (90) have an average of 70 — neither equals 70 so no change. The trap only fires in an industry where an account sits exactly at the mean. Shipping code with >= instead of > is silently wrong whenever that edge case exists.\n\n**Sanity check:** Confirm 4 rows. Verify the 4 companies are Kilo Pharma (250), Foxtrot Media (40), Juliet Fashion (90), and Echo Tech (300). Cross-check: tech avg = (150+300+100)/3 = 183.3 — only Echo Tech (300) and Acme Corp (150) are above this; November SaaS (100) is below. Wait — Acme Corp (150) < 183.3, only Echo Tech (300) > 183.3. Confirm only 1 tech account appears.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** \'The average shifts as accounts are added — should each account be compared to the live average or a fixed benchmark, and why?\'',
    sqliteNote: null,
