    checkValues: [{ user_id: '4', email: 'dan@example.com' }],
    solution: 'SELECT DISTINCT u.email, u.user_id\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o1 ON u.user_id = o1.user_id AND o1.status = \'returned\'\nJOIN orders o2 ON u.user_id = o2.user_id AND o2.status = \'completed\'\n  AND o2.created_at > o1.created_at\nORDER BY u.user_id',
    debrief: 'Users 4 (returned Apr 2023, re-purchased Nov 2023) and 10 (returned Jul 2023, re-purchased Jan 2024) qualify. User 12 does not — their completed order (Aug 2023) came before their return (Apr 2024), making it a new dissatisfaction, not a recovery. The temporal condition AND o2.created_at > o1.created_at is essential: without it, a user who ordered, then returned, then never came back again would still appear (they have both a returned and a completed order, just in the wrong order). DISTINCT handles cases where a user has multiple returns or completions that produce Cartesian product rows from the double join. Alternative approach: two EXISTS correlated subqueries — one for any returned order, one for any completed order dated after the return — is more readable but requires the same temporal logic.\n\n**Forensic trap:** A junior analyst drops the temporal condition and writes JOIN orders o2 ON u.user_id = o2.user_id AND o2.status = \'completed\' without AND o2.created_at > o1.created_at. This returns users 4, 10, AND 12 — three rows instead of two. User 12 has a completed order (Aug 2023) and a returned order (Apr 2024), so both joins match. The output looks plausible because three recovery customers feels like a reasonable number for a mid-sized catalog.\n\n**Sanity check:** For each returned user in the result, manually verify that at least one completed order date exceeds at least one returned order date. User 12\'s completed order (2023-08) < their returned order (2024-04) — if they appear in the output, the temporal condition is missing.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** \'How would you require the re-purchase to come after the return, and within 90 days, to capture genuine win-back?\'',
