    datamartId: 'consumer',
    prompt: 'The engagement team at YouTube needs interaction counts broken down by action, plus a final TOTAL row so the numbers visibly add up. Return action and count, with one extra row where action is \'TOTAL\' holding the overall interaction count. Order by count descending.',
    expectedColumns: ['action', 'count'],
    expectedRowCount: 6,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider how to group interactions by action type and calculate the count for each. Also think about how to add a total row at the top of the results.', starterCode: 'SELECT action, COUNT(*) AS count FROM interactions GROUP BY action;' },
      { text: 'You need to combine the grouped results with a total row. Think about using UNION ALL to merge these two sets of data.', starterCode: 'SELECT action, COUNT(*) AS count FROM interactions GROUP BY action\nUNION ALL\nSELECT \'TOTAL\', COUNT(*) FROM interactions;' }
    ],
    hints: ['What does one row in your result represent? Your output needs 6 rows with columns: action, count.', 'Every column in SELECT that is not inside an aggregate must appear in GROUP BY. Sketch the output shape first — one row per what?'],
    checkValues: [{ action: 'TOTAL', count: '30' }],
    solution: 'SELECT action, COUNT(*) AS count\nFROM interactions\nGROUP BY action\nUNION ALL\nSELECT \'TOTAL\', COUNT(*)\nFROM interactions\nORDER BY 2 DESC',
    debrief: 'What the stakeholder wants: A summary of interactions per action type with a TOTAL row appended, so QA can cross-check that per-action counts sum to the overall count.\n\nAmbiguities resolved: The total is a separate summary row, not a column. The result has one row per action type plus one TOTAL row — 6 rows in this dataset. The TOTAL row is for QA and display; it should not be used for downstream aggregation.\n\nSQL approach: GROUP BY action gives the per-action breakdown. UNION ALL appends a separate SELECT COUNT(*) row labeled \'TOTAL\'. ORDER BY 2 DESC sorts by count descending, so TOTAL (the largest number) appears first. ORDER BY references the second column positionally — alias-based ORDER BY may not resolve consistently across UNION branches.\n\nWhat weak SQL looks like: Using UNION instead of UNION ALL — UNION deduplicates, and if any action type happened to have the same count as TOTAL it would be silently dropped. Or GROUP BY ROLLUP — cleaner in PostgreSQL but not standard in SQLite; interviewers may want the UNION ALL pattern explicitly.\n\nInterviewer follow-up: \'How would you add a percentage column showing each action type\'s share of total interactions?\'\n\n**Forensic trap:** A junior analyst writes UNION instead of UNION ALL. In the consumer datamart, the \'view\' action has 14 interactions. The TOTAL row is SELECT \'TOTAL\', COUNT(*) FROM interactions = 49 — a distinct value from all action counts. So UNION deduplication does not drop any row here and the output looks identical to the correct answer. The trap fires silently: if a future dataset happened to have an action type with the same count as TOTAL, that action row would be dropped without error.\n\n**Sanity check:** The per-action counts (14+9+9+8+9 = 49) should sum exactly to the TOTAL row value. Add up the non-TOTAL rows and compare to the TOTAL row — if they differ, UNION deduplication has dropped a row.',
    sqliteNote: null,
