    id: 'sql-h53',
    title: 'Courier Workload vs Delivery Output',
    company: 'Uber Eats',
    companyDomain: 'ubereats.com',
    difficulty: 'Hard',
    isFree: false,
    tags: ['COUNT', 'CASE WHEN', 'conditional aggregation', 'two interpretations', 'delivery analytics'],
    roles: ['PA', 'DA', 'PM'],
    priority: 1,
    estimatedMin: 18,
    datamartId: 'food_delivery',
    prompt: 'The Uber Eats fleet team needs a performance report for couriers. The report should include courier_id, total_dispatched (all orders assigned to the courier), and total_delivered (orders with status = \'delivered\'). Exclude orders with no courier assigned. Order by courier_id.',
    expectedColumns: ['courier_id', 'total_dispatched', 'total_delivered'],
    expectedRowCount: 7,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'You need to calculate two metrics for each courier: total dispatched orders and total delivered orders. Focus on the orders table as it contains the necessary status information.', starterCode: 'SELECT courier_id FROM orders WHERE courier_id IS NOT NULL;' },
      { text: 'Use conditional aggregation to count dispatched orders and delivered orders separately. Remember that dispatched orders include all assigned orders, while delivered orders are filtered by status.', starterCode: 'SELECT courier_id, COUNT(*) AS total_dispatched FROM orders WHERE courier_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY courier_id;' },
      { text: 'To calculate total_delivered, use a CASE WHEN statement to count only orders with status = \'delivered\'. Combine this with the total_dispatched calculation.', starterCode: 'SELECT courier_id, COUNT(*) AS total_dispatched, SUM(CASE WHEN status = \'delivered\' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS total_delivered FROM orders WHERE courier_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY courier_id;' },
      { text: 'Ensure that you exclude any orders where the courier_id is null. This will be handled in the WHERE clause of your query.', starterCode: 'SELECT courier_id, COUNT(*) AS total_dispatched, SUM(CASE WHEN status = \'delivered\' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS total_delivered FROM orders WHERE courier_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY courier_id ORDER BY courier_id;' },
      { text: 'Finally, sort the results by courier_id to meet the ordering requirement in the problem statement.', starterCode: 'SELECT courier_id, COUNT(*) AS total_dispatched, SUM(CASE WHEN status = \'delivered\' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS total_delivered FROM orders WHERE courier_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY courier_id ORDER BY courier_id;' }
    ],
    hints: ['What does one row in your result represent? Your output needs 8 rows with columns: courier_id, total_dispatched, total_delivered.', 'CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END works like if-else inside SQL. You can use it inside aggregate functions for conditional counts.', 'Break the problem down: write the core SELECT/FROM/WHERE first, then add aggregation.', 'Double-check your column aliases match the expected output column names exactly.', 'Before finalising, confirm: does your query return exactly 8 rows? Run it and check the row count first, then verify specific values.'],
    checkValues: [{ courier_id: '1', total_dispatched: '4', total_delivered: '3' }, { courier_id: '2', total_dispatched: '4', total_delivered: '4' }],
    solution: 'SELECT courier_id,\n  COUNT(*) AS total_dispatched,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = \'delivered\' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS total_delivered\nFROM orders\nWHERE courier_id IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY courier_id\nORDER BY courier_id',
    debrief: 'Two valid metrics that answer different questions: total_dispatched is workload, total_delivered is output. Courier 3 was assigned 5 orders and completed 4 (an 80% delivery rate); courier 1, 4 assigned and 3 delivered; courier 5, 3 and 2. The gap between the columns is the cancellation signal the fleet team cares about.\n\n**The mistake that runs and erases the question:** adding WHERE status = \'delivered\'. Now every order counted is already delivered, so total_dispatched and total_delivered come out identical — courier 3 reads 4 and 4, courier 1 reads 3 and 3 — and the workload-vs-output gap vanishes. The report runs and looks clean, but it can no longer show that courier 3 dropped one of five. **Catch it:** if total_dispatched equals total_delivered for every courier, you filtered out the undelivered orders you were supposed to measure. Keep the condition inside SUM(CASE WHEN status = \'delivered\' ...), not in WHERE.\n\n**Sanity check:** dispatched >= delivered for every courier; courier 3 = 5 dispatched / 4 delivered; orders with NULL courier_id excluded.\n\n**Before writing:** ask whether they want workload or output — if unspecified, return both, as here.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** \'How would you turn this into a delivery-success rate, and keep a courier with two dispatches from topping the list at 100%?\'',
