    checkValues: [{ dept_name: 'Backend', total_headcount: '3', active_headcount: '3' }],
    solution: 'SELECT d.name AS dept_name,\n  COUNT(e.emp_id) AS total_headcount,\n  SUM(e.is_active) AS active_headcount\nFROM departments d\nLEFT JOIN employees e ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id\nGROUP BY d.name\nORDER BY d.name',
    debrief: '\'Headcount\' is one of the most commonly undefined metrics in HR. Definition A (everyone on record) totals 15; Definition B (active only) totals 14 — Sales shows 2 total but 1 active because Ivan Petrov is terminated (is_active = 0). Returning both columns lets the CFO pick a definition without asking for a second query.\n\n**The mistake that runs and under-reports to the board:** filtering WHERE is_active = 1 and presenting the single number as \'headcount\'. It runs and returns a clean 14, but it silently commits to one definition — the board never sees that 15 people are on record, and Sales looks like a one-person team. **Catch it:** a single-number headcount should prompt the question \'active or on-record?\'; if total and active can differ (a terminated employee exists), one number is hiding a decision.\n\n**Sanity check:** total_headcount sums to 15, active to 14; every department has total >= active; Sales is the only one where they differ (2 vs 1).\n\n**Mechanics:** LEFT JOIN from departments keeps a department even with zero employees; SUM(is_active) works because is_active is 0/1 (COUNT(CASE WHEN is_active = 1 ...) is the verbose equivalent).\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** \'If the board also wants headcount as of a past date, what would you need beyond an is_active flag?\'',
