    solution: 'SELECT dispute_id, amount, opened_at\nFROM disputes\nWHERE resolved_at IS NULL\nORDER BY amount DESC',
    debrief: 'The bug is = NULL instead of IS NULL. In SQL, NULL represents an unknown value. Any comparison using = against NULL evaluates to NULL (unknown), not TRUE or FALSE — so WHERE resolved_at = NULL never matches any row. IS NULL is the only correct predicate for filtering NULL values.\n\n**Why it passed review:** The query ran, returned results (an empty table), and an empty compliance queue looks like resolved disputes, not broken SQL. The output was not an error — it was a plausible business outcome.\n\n**This is the most operationally dangerous forensic class:** an empty result that means "all clear" when the correct result means "urgent action required." The $3,500 dispute (dispute 6) sat unreviewed for two weeks.\n\n**Catch it:** Before deploying any dashboard that uses a NULL filter, run SELECT COUNT(*) FROM disputes WHERE resolved_at IS NULL directly. If that returns > 0 but your dashboard shows 0, the filter is broken.',
