    solution: 'SELECT ROUND(AVG(subtotal), 2) AS avg_order_value\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = \'delivered\'',
    debrief: 'The bug is averaging per-customer averages (a two-step aggregation) instead of computing a single average across all orders. Customer 1 has 5 delivered orders averaging $32.82. Customer 11 has 1 order at $13.50. The broken query gives both customers equal weight — so the $13.50 order suppresses the average as much as five $33 orders. The correct AOV is $28.47, not $27.00.\n\n**Why the wrong approach feels right:** "normalize by customer to avoid power users skewing the metric" is a reasonable instinct for some metrics (like engagement rate). For AOV it is wrong — every order is an equal business transaction regardless of who placed it.\n\n**The rule:** Average of averages is only mathematically valid when all groups are the same size. When group sizes differ, you need a weighted average: total_revenue / total_orders. Always check whether your denominator is "orders" or "customers" before aggregating.\n\n**Where this matters:** AOV is a key input to LTV calculations. A 5% understatement in AOV compounds across the entire LTV model. If the finance team is building budget forecasts on $27 instead of $28.47, the cumulative error is significant.',
