    solution: 'SELECT user_id, duration_min AS first_session_duration\nFROM (\n  SELECT user_id, duration_min,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY user_id\n      ORDER BY session_date ASC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM sessions\n)\nWHERE rn = 1\nORDER BY user_id',
    debrief: 'The bug is ORDER BY session_date DESC inside the ROW_NUMBER window. DESC sorts most-recent first, so rn=1 picks the last session. The fix is ASC — earliest date gets rn=1.\n\n**Why the wrong output looks right:** first-session durations for retained users are often shorter than mature-player sessions. The inflated numbers (61 min for user 1, 110 min for user 6) look like a sign of strong engagement. Nobody questioned a metric that told a positive story.\n\n**The data impact:** user 1\'s true first session was 42 min. Their last was 61 min. User 6\'s first session was 90 min; their last was 110 min. The onboarding team has been benchmarking against late-lifecycle sessions for three months, making new users\' actual first sessions look underperforming by comparison.\n\n**Window function direction check:** whenever you use ROW_NUMBER() to find a "first" or "last" record, verify: ASC = first (smallest date/value at rn=1). DESC = last (largest date/value at rn=1). This is one of the most common window function bugs in production analytics.',
