    datamartId: 'gaming',
    prompt: 'Supercell\'s investor deck reports an ARPU of $18.73, but the data analyst notices it matches the average revenue per paying user instead of total users. The platform has 12 registered users, with only 4 making purchases. Identify the bug in the ARPU calculation.',
    brokenQuery: 'SELECT ROUND(AVG(total_spent), 2) AS arpu\nFROM (\n  SELECT u.user_id, SUM(p.amount) AS total_spent\n  FROM users u\n  LEFT JOIN purchases p ON u.user_id = p.user_id\n  GROUP BY u.user_id\n)',
    brokenOutputNote: 'Returns $18.73 — the average revenue per paying user, not per total user. The LEFT JOIN correctly includes all 12 users, but non-paying users have total_spent = NULL. AVG() silently ignores NULLs, so only the 4 paying users\' totals enter the calculation. The denominator is 4, not 12.',
    expectedColumns: ['arpu'],
    expectedRowCount: 1,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider how the ARPU calculation is being affected by users who have not made any purchases. Think about how to handle missing data in the aggregation.', starterCode: 'SELECT u.user_id, COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS total_spent FROM users u LEFT JOIN purchases p ON u.user_id = p.user_id GROUP BY u.user_id;' },
      { text: 'Examine how the average is being computed. The current approach might be averaging over all users, including those with no purchases, which could skew the result.', starterCode: 'SELECT ROUND(AVG(total_spent), 2) AS arpu FROM ( SELECT u.user_id, COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS total_spent FROM users u LEFT JOIN purchases p ON u.user_id = p.user_id GROUP BY u.user_id ) AS subquery;' }
    ],
    hints: ['What does AVG() do when it encounters NULL values?', 'How do you convert a NULL SUM to 0 so non-paying users are counted in the average?'],
    checkValues: [{ arpu: '6.24' }],
    solution: 'SELECT ROUND(AVG(total_spent), 2) AS arpu\nFROM (\n  SELECT u.user_id, COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) AS total_spent\n  FROM users u\n  LEFT JOIN purchases p ON u.user_id = p.user_id\n  GROUP BY u.user_id\n)',
    debrief: 'The bug is missing COALESCE. SUM(p.amount) returns NULL for users with no purchases (because SUM of no rows is NULL). AVG() skips NULLs, so the denominator shrinks from 12 to 4. The broken ARPU is $74.90 / 4 = $18.73. The correct ARPU is $74.90 / 12 = $6.24 — a 3× overstatement.\n\n**The fix:** COALESCE(SUM(p.amount), 0) converts NULL to 0 for non-paying users. Now AVG has 12 values: four purchase totals and eight zeros. The correct ARPU is $6.24.\n\n**Why this matters for investor reporting:** ARPU is a standardized metric that investors compare across companies. Reporting $18.73 when the correct number is $6.24 overstates monetization efficiency by 3×. If an investor calculates LTV using this ARPU, their model will be significantly wrong.\n\n**The general rule:** LEFT JOIN + AVG is almost always buggy without COALESCE. The LEFT JOIN is there to include non-matching rows, but AVG ignores them unless you explicitly convert NULL to a numeric value. Whenever you write LEFT JOIN followed by an aggregate, ask: "what should non-matching rows contribute to this calculation?"',
