    debrief: 'The bug is COUNT(*) vs COUNT(DISTINCT user_id). COUNT(*) counts rows — there are 20 level_attempt rows. COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts each player once regardless of how many attempts they made, returning 6. Only users 1, 3, 5, 6, 9, and 10 have any level attempts.\n\n**The impossibility check:** unique_players can never exceed SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users = 12. Any value greater than 12 is impossible and immediately signals COUNT(*) was used instead of COUNT(DISTINCT). This is the same class of bug as f03 (buyer count) — the number exceeds its natural ceiling.\n\n**Why attempts and players diverge here:** levels 5 and 10 are hard (multiple failed attempts before passing). User 1 attempts level 5 three times. User 3 attempts 5 different levels across multiple sessions. In a game with difficult progression gates, the attempt-to-player ratio inflates fast — making COUNT(*) a dangerous default.\n\n**The naming trap:** the column is aliased unique_players, which sounds like it should return unique players. But COUNT(*) returns anything but unique counts. Always verify your COUNT expression matches what the alias claims.',
