    datamartId: 'ecomm',
    prompt: 'The retention team at Etsy needs a report that shows all users along with their 2024 order count, including those who didn\'t place any orders. This will help identify inactive accounts for a win-back campaign. The query should return user_id, email, and 2024 order count. The report currently returns only 7 rows despite there being 15 registered users. ',
    brokenQuery: 'SELECT u.user_id, u.email, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders_2024\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON u.user_id = o.user_id\nWHERE o.created_at >= \'2024-01-01\'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.email\nORDER BY u.user_id',
    brokenOutputNote: 'Returns 7 rows instead of 15. The WHERE clause filters on a column from the right (orders) table. For users with no 2024 orders, the LEFT JOIN produces NULL for o.created_at — but WHERE o.created_at >= \'2024-01-01\' evaluates to FALSE for NULL, dropping those users. The LEFT JOIN becomes an INNER JOIN in effect.',
    expectedColumns: ['user_id', 'email', 'orders_2024'],
    expectedRowCount: 15,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider how the LEFT JOIN is affecting the count of orders for users. Ensure that all users, including those with no 2024 orders, are being included in the result.', starterCode: 'SELECT u.user_id, u.email, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders_2024\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON u.user_id = o.user_id\n  AND o.created_at >= \'2024-01-01\'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.email\nORDER BY u.user_id;' },
      { text: 'Check if the filtering condition on created_at is inadvertently excluding some users or orders. Ensure that the logic correctly captures all 2024 orders for each user.', starterCode: 'SELECT u.user_id, u.email, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders_2024\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON u.user_id = o.user_id\n  AND o.created_at >= \'2024-01-01\'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.email\nORDER BY u.user_id;' }
    ],
    hints: ['What happens when you filter on a right-table column in WHERE after a LEFT JOIN?', 'Where should the date filter go so it doesn\'t eliminate non-matching left-table rows?'],
    checkValues: [{ user_id: '1', orders_2024: '1' }],
    solution: 'SELECT u.user_id, u.email, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders_2024\nFROM users u\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON u.user_id = o.user_id\n  AND o.created_at >= \'2024-01-01\'\nGROUP BY u.user_id, u.email\nORDER BY u.user_id',
