    solution: 'SELECT ROUND(AVG(seller_txns), 1) AS avg_completed_txns\nFROM (\n  SELECT s.seller_id,\n    COUNT(CASE WHEN t.status = \'completed\' THEN 1 END) AS seller_txns\n  FROM sellers s\n  LEFT JOIN transactions t ON s.seller_id = t.seller_id\n  GROUP BY s.seller_id\n)',
    debrief: 'The bug is survivorship bias: the inner subquery groups by seller_id from the transactions table, which only includes sellers who have at least one transaction row. Sellers with zero completed transactions are not in the transactions table as a "completed" row — so they don\'t appear in the GROUP BY result at all. They survive in the sellers table but are invisible to the metric.\n\n**The math:** 9 sellers made at least one completed sale, totaling 23 completed transactions. Broken avg = 23/9 = 2.6. But seller 6 (GadgetWorld, joined July 2023) has only a refunded transaction — zero completed sales. Correct avg = 23/10 = 2.3.\n\n**Why survivorship bias matters here:** the broken metric of 2.6 implies a healthy engaged seller base. The correct 2.3 — combined with the fact that 10% of sellers have zero completed transactions — paints a different picture. In a larger marketplace, this pattern compounds: if 20% of registered sellers have zero sales, the broken metric overstates average engagement by 25%.\n\n**The structural fix:** always start from the entity table (sellers, users, products) and LEFT JOIN to activity tables. This guarantees every entity is represented, even those with zero activity. Aggregating from the activity table directly, then averaging, systematically excludes the zero-activity population.\n\n**The detection rule:** if your average engagement metric looks higher than what your account managers report from their individual dashboards, check whether zero-activity entities are included in the denominator.',
