    debrief: 'COUNT(*) counts every row in the group — including multiple events from the same user. For login, 8 unique users collectively triggered 24 login events. The query names the column unique_user_count but computes raw event volume, which is a completely different metric.\n\n**The trap:** The column name says unique_user_count and the query uses GROUP BY event_name — both look correct at a glance. The mistake is in the aggregation function: COUNT(*) instead of COUNT(DISTINCT user_id). The output for invite_sent reveals the bug most clearly: 3 invite_sent events but only 2 unique users (user 14 sent two invites).\n\n**Why this matters:** Feature reach and feature usage are different metrics. Reach tells you how many users have tried a feature. Usage tells you how many times. Reporting 24 "unique users" who logged in when only 8 users exist inflates the DAU story and misrepresents product engagement in executive dashboards.\n\n**Sanity check:** Check any event where unique_user_count exceeds the total number of users in the accounts table. That\'s impossible if the count is genuinely unique — an immediate signal that COUNT(*) was used.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** "If the same user logged in 5 times today and 0 times yesterday, how do you count them in a DAU metric — and would you use COUNT(*) or COUNT(DISTINCT user_id)?"',
