    estimatedMin: 7,
    datamartId: 'fintech',
    prompt: 'Stripe\'s compliance team needs to identify all transactions associated with high-risk users to ensure adherence to regulatory standards. The query should return transaction IDs, amounts, and occurred_at dates for transactions linked to users with a risk_tier of \'high\'. Ensure the query handles potential subquery issues and returns valid results. ',
    brokenQuery: 'SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.category, t.occurred_at\nFROM transactions t\nWHERE t.account_id = (\n  SELECT a.account_id\n  FROM accounts a\n  WHERE a.user_id IN (\n    SELECT u.user_id FROM users u WHERE u.risk_tier = \'high\'\n  )\n)\nORDER BY t.txn_id',
    brokenOutputNote: 'SQLite error: "sub-select returns 2 rows". The inner subquery returns account_ids for two high-risk users — but the = operator expects exactly one value.',
    expectedColumns: ['txn_id', 'amount', 'category', 'occurred_at'],
    expectedRowCount: 2,
    hintSteps: [
      { text: 'Consider the structure of the nested subquery and how it\'s being used in the IN clause. Ensure that the subquery is returning a single column of values.', starterCode: 'SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.category, t.occurred_at FROM transactions t WHERE t.account_id IN ( SELECT a.account_id FROM accounts a WHERE a.user_id IN ( SELECT u.user_id FROM users u ) ) ORDER BY t.txn_id' },
      { text: 'Check if the subquery is returning multiple rows or an incorrect data type. Ensure that the nested query aligns with the expected input for the IN clause.', starterCode: 'SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.category, t.occurred_at FROM transactions t WHERE t.account_id IN ( SELECT a.account_id FROM accounts a WHERE a.user_id IN ( SELECT u.user_id FROM users u WHERE u.risk_tier = \'high\' ) ) ORDER BY t.txn_id' }
    ],
    hints: ['The = operator is a scalar comparison — it expects one value on the right. What operator accepts a list of values?', 'There are two high-risk users in the system. Their account IDs both need to be matched.'],
    checkValues: [{ txn_id: '10', amount: '45' }, { txn_id: '20', amount: '780' }],
    solution: 'SELECT t.txn_id, t.amount, t.category, t.occurred_at\nFROM transactions t\nWHERE t.account_id IN (\n  SELECT a.account_id\n  FROM accounts a\n  WHERE a.user_id IN (\n    SELECT u.user_id FROM users u WHERE u.risk_tier = \'high\'\n  )\n)\nORDER BY t.txn_id',
    debrief: 'The = operator is a scalar comparison. It expects exactly one value on the right side. The subquery SELECT a.account_id FROM accounts WHERE user_id IN (...) returns two account_ids — one for each high-risk user — causing SQLite to throw "sub-select returns 2 rows."\n\n**The fix:** Replace = with IN. The IN operator accepts a subquery that returns a set of values and checks whether the left side matches any of them. One-character change, semantically correct for a multi-row subquery.\n\n**Why this pattern is dangerous in production:** The query might have worked correctly during development if only one high-risk user existed at the time. The bug only surfaces when a second high-risk user is added — making it a time-bomb that passes code review and breaks in production.\n\n**The correct result:** Two transactions. Transaction 10 ($45, account 6, user dan — high-risk, frozen account). Transaction 20 ($780, account 12, user iris — high-risk, closed account, rejected KYC). Both are compliance-critical records.\n\n**Sanity check:** After fixing = to IN, verify exactly 2 rows are returned. Cross-check: users with risk_tier = \'high\' are users 4 and 9. Their accounts are 6 and 12. Transactions for those accounts are txn 10 and txn 20.\n\n**Interviewer follow-up:** "In production with millions of users, a subquery for risk_tier = \'high\' could return thousands of account_ids. What\'s a more performant alternative to the nested IN approach?"',
